      "prompt_id": "short_03",
      "tier": "short",
      "prompt": "What is the difference between TCP and UDP? Give a concrete example use case for each.",
      "prompt_tokens": 47,
      "output_tokens": 256,
      "output_text": "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol) are both protocols used in the transport layer of the TCP/IP model, but they differ significantly in their approach to data transmission and reliability.\n\n### TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)\nTCP is a connection-oriented protocol, which means it establishes a connection before data is sent. This connection is set up through a three-way handshake process. Once the connection is established, TCP ensures that the data is delivered reliably, in order, and without loss. It also handles flow control and congestion control to ensure that the network is not overloaded.\n\n**Example Use Case:**\n- **Web Browsing:** When you visit a website, your browser sends HTTP requests to the server. The server responds with HTML content, which is then sent back to your browser. This entire process involves multiple TCP connections, ensuring that each piece of data is delivered correctly and in the right order.\n\n### UDP (User Datagram Protocol)\nUDP is a connectionless protocol, which means it does not establish a connection before sending data. Instead, it sends data directly to the destination. UDP does not guarantee delivery, order, or reliability of the data. It is faster and more lightweight than TCP, but it is not suitable for applications that require these guarantees.\n\n**Example Use",
